hadiths[2]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1361","Muhammad bin Yahya told us, Abdullah bin Yazid Al-Muqri told us, Haywa told us, Abu Al-Aswad told us that he heard Urwa bin Al-Zubayr narrates, on the authority of Marwan bin Al-Hakam, that he asked Abu Hurairah: Did you pray the prayer of fear with the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace? Abu Hurairah said: Yes, he said: When? He said: “It was the year of the Battle of Najd, so the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, stood up for the afternoon prayer, and one group of people stood up with him and another group opposite them.” The enemy, with their backs to the Qiblah, so the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said “Allahu Akbar,” and all those with him and those facing the enemy said “Allahu Akbar” with him. Then the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, kneeled one rak’ah, and the group that followed him kneeled with him, then he prostrated and the group that followed him prostrated, The others stood up from those following the enemy, then the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, rose up and the group following him rose up, so they went to the enemy and faced them. The group that was facing the enemy came and they knelt down and prostrated while the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, was standing as he was. Then they got up and the Messenger of God knelt down. God, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, performed another rak’ah, so they knelt with him and prostrated with him, then the group that was facing the enemy came and they knelt down and prostrated. The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, was sitting and those with him, and then there was peace, and the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, greeted them all, so it was for the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace. May God’s prayers and peace be upon him, two rak’ahs, and for every man of the two sects two rak’ahs, two rak’ahs.”"
"1362","Abu Al-Azhar told us, and I wrote it down from its origins. Ya’qub bin Ibrahim told us, my father told us, on the authority of Ibn Ishaq, Muhammad bin Abd told me. Al-Rahman bin Al-Aswad bin Nawfal, and he was an orphan in the care of Urwa bin Al-Zubayr, and he is one of the sons of Asad bin Abdul-Uzza bin Qusayy, on the authority of Urwah ibn al-Zubayr said: I heard Abu Hurairah and Marwan ibn al-Hakam asking him about the fear prayer. Abu Hurairah said: I was with The Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, in that battle, said: “Then the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, split two holes with the people, and he mentioned the hadith. With a similar meaning, and he mentioned it in the second rak’ah, he said: “The group that prayed behind him took their weapons, then they walked backwards on They turned back until they stood up from the enemy’s direction,” and he added at the end of the hadith, “Then the people stood up and joined him in prayer.”"
